              "ImageDescription": {
                "value": "Tagging operation of an albacore tuna.</p><p class=\"mw-empty-elt\"></p><p> The yellowfin tuna, also known as the yellowfin tuna, is a species of fish with a cosmopolitan distribution: in fact, it is found globally in the Western Atlantic, the Red Sea and the Indo-Pacific region (particularly in East Africa and around New Zealand). It lives in schools in waters close to the surface, at temperatures ranging from 18 to 31°C. This species measures on average 150 cm when adult. It is recognizable, among other things, by its yellow fins (which gave it its vernacular name).</p><p class=\"mw-empty-elt\"></p><p> Appreciated for its flesh, particularly by European consumers, yellowfin tuna remains a resource to be monitored: in fact, the yellowfin tuna population in the Indian Ocean is threatened by overfishing. There were 430,327 tonnes of yellowfin tuna caught in 2014 (source: Indian Ocean Tuna Commission).",
